A site like sourceforge.net allows for management of all aspects of a
good development process, including testing.  That site has a fine
pedigree for producing some good software.  In the hands of a good
manager, nethack could still be under a central control, but all the
benefits of the site's organizational and communication tools would
also come to bear.  Any worries about YANIs dragging the game into the
gutter are unfounded if the project is under good management.  I
personally don't even care if the current "devteam" manages the
project; I just want them committed to responding to bugs,
communication, and release on a schedule that isn't lethargic.  Most
people don't like transparency and commitment, though.


